## Contracts
The following conditions are treated as data contracts:

- PLR identifier
	- present
	- non-null
	- unique
- geometry:
	- present
	- not empty
	- valid 
	- polygonal (i.e. a Planungsraum cannot be a point or line)
- the current source version must use EPSG:25833
- the current LOR snapshot must contain 542 PLRs

The last contract implicitly checks for changes to the LOR geometry.

# ICON-D2 static grid
## Source meaning
[Source link](http://icon-downloads.mpimet.mpg.de/dwd_grids.xml#grid47)

The ICON-D2 grid is the spatial grid on which DWD model output is provided. The project uses DWD Grid #47, stored in `data/raw/icon-d2-grid/icon_grid_0047_R19B07_L.nc.bz2`.


The grid definition contains cell centers, grid vertices, and the vertex-to-cell connectivity required to reconstruct the native triangular cells.

## Observed structural values

The grid used by the project contains:

- cells: {{ grid.cell_count }}
- vertices: {{ grid.vertex_count }}
- vertices per cell: 3 (by definition)

These values were confirmed while decoding the grid and are used as current grid-version expectations.

## Profiling

The grid profiler records:

- NetCDF dimensions
- available variables
- number of cells
- number of vertices
- normalized connectivity shape
- number of unique vertices referenced

## Contracts

The current grid contracts are:

- required NetCDF variables must be present
- `clon` and `clat` must have compatible lengths
- `vlon` and `vlat` must have compatible lengths
- the current grid contains 542,040 cells
- the current grid contains 272,089 vertices
- every cell must reference exactly three vertices
- all connectivity indexes must reference valid vertices
- longitude and latitude coordinates must be finite
- longitude must remain within valid geographic bounds
- latitude must remain within valid geographic bounds

The cell and vertex counts are version-specific expectations for the grid used by the project.

# ICON-D2-RUC T_2M
## Source
[Source link](https://dwd-geoportal.de/products/iconruc/)

The dynamic environmental input is the ICON-D2-RUC `T_2M` field.

Each decoded run is represented as one temperature value per native ICON grid cell, together with:

```{text}
cell_index
run_time_utc
valid_time_utc
temperature_k
temperature_c
```

Architecture 1 currently works with lead-zero fields, so the run time and valid time are expected to match.

## Missing-value semantics

During profiling, I discovered temperature values of 9999 Kelvin and 9725.85 Celsius . I assume that these denote missing values. I report missing values separately for the entire dataset and for Berlin-relevant cells.

## Profiling

Each T_2M run produces a separate profiling report.

The profiler records:

- total row count
- distinct cell count
- duplicate cell indexes
- null counts
- run time
- valid time
- forecast lead time
- missing-temperature count
- missing-temperature share
- observed temperature distribution in Kelvin
- observed temperature distribution in Celsius
- non-finite observed-value counts
- Berlin-relevant cell coverage
- missing temperatures among Berlin-relevant cells

Observed temperature distributions are profiling information and are not expected to remain constant between model runs.

## Contracts

The T_2M contracts are:

- required columns must exist
- the field must contain 542,040 rows for the current ICON grid
- `cell_index` must be non-null
- `cell_index` must be unique
- exactly one run time must occur in a file
- exactly one valid time must occur in a file
- for the lead-zero field, run time must equal valid time
- missing temperatures must use the consistent pair `9999 K / 9725.85 °C`
- non-missing Kelvin values must be finite
- non-missing Celsius values must be finite
- Celsius must equal Kelvin minus 273.15 within numerical tolerance
- observed Kelvin temperatures must lie within the project's broad sanity range of 253 K to 323 K

I used the 253–323 K (approx. −20 °C to 50 °C) range to identify unusual values during manual profiling. I am keeping it as a broad sanity check, to detect decoding failures or corrupt values. It is not meant to define climatologically normal weather.

# ICON-D2 ↔ Planungsraum area bridge
## 6.1 Purpose
The ICON-D2 weather grid and the Berlin LOR geometry use different spatial units. The project therefore derives a static bridge between ICON triangles and Planungsräume by calculating polygon intersections. The bridge uses polygon intersection.

Each bridge row contains:

```{text}
plr_id
cell_index
intersection_area_m2
plr_area_m2
icon_cell_area_m2
fraction_of_plr
fraction_of_icon_cell
```

`fraction_of_plr` represents the share of a Planungsraum covered by a particular ICON cell and is used for area-weighted PLR temperature aggregation.

## Observed current bridge
The current bridge build produced:

- Planungsräume: {{ bridge.distinct_plr_count }}
- ICON cells intersecting Berlin: {{ bridge.distinct_icon_cell_count }}
- PLR ↔ ICON intersection rows: {{ bridge.row_count }}
- mean ICON cells per PLR: {{ bridge.icon_cells_per_plr.mean }}
- median ICON cells per PLR: {{ bridge.icon_cells_per_plr.median }}
- maximum ICON cells per PLR: {{ bridge.icon_cells_per_plr.max }}

The generated bridge covered every PLR completely within numerical precision.

## Profiling

The bridge profiler records:

- number of intersection rows
- distinct PLR count
- distinct ICON cell count
- duplicate `(plr_id, cell_index)` pairs
- intersection-area distribution
- `fraction_of_plr` distribution
- `fraction_of_icon_cell` distribution
- number of ICON cells per PLR
- sum of `fraction_of_plr` per PLR
- total spatial coverage per PLR
- missing source PLR IDs
- unexpected PLR IDs
- bridge ICON cells missing from the decoded ICON grid

## Contracts

The bridge contracts are:

- required columns must exist
- every current LOR PLR must appear in the bridge
- every bridge `cell_index` must exist in the ICON grid
- every bridge `plr_id` must exist in the LOR dataset
- `(plr_id, cell_index)` pairs must be unique
- intersection areas must be positive
- `fraction_of_plr` must be greater than zero
- `fraction_of_icon_cell` must be greater than zero
- fractions must not exceed one beyond floating-point tolerance
- `fraction_of_plr` must sum to approximately 1.0 for each PLR
- summed intersection area must cover approximately 100% of each PLR

A synthetic geometry unit test also verifies the area-weighting principle independently of the Berlin data by constructing a known geometry split and checking that the resulting fractions sum correctly.

# NB: Floating-point behavior in spatial calculations
Spatial intersection areas and ratios are represented using floating-point numbers. During bridge testing, at least one fraction failed a test because I used the exact comparisons `fraction <= 1`. I changed tests involving spatial ratios to use a small numerical tolerance instead of exact equality.


